00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
in indonesia's northern aceh province a  perilous and exhausting journey has  finally come to an end for these  rohingya refugees their boat which had  114 people on board had set sail from a  refugee camp in bangladesh 25 days ago  indonesian fishermen saw it land on a  beach in the early hours of sunday  morning and called the police  people here are giving them food and  shelter while the authorities complete  health checks including covid19 tests  we'll leave it to the government but  we're helping them because it's the  humane thing to do if we don't receive  them where can they go  indonesian aid workers are also helping  they say some refugees are ill after  their long journey  some of them are not feeling well one  refugee has swellings on her arms and  legs and is unable to stand or walk  while another is malnourished probably  because he's been at sea for too long  hundreds of rohingya who fled a military  offensive in myanmar have reached  indonesia in recent years but indonesia  regards itself as only a transit country  where refugees cannot permanently  resettle  so for these rohingya the danger is over  for now but the uncertainty remains as  to what their future holds victoria  gatenby al jazeera
 

